<header class="ahead band" id="top"><div class="wrap">
  <p class="ahead__meta"><span>Feb 2, 2026</span><i>·</i>
    <a href="/category/hiring-philosophy">Hiring philosophy</a><i>·</i><span>4 min read</span></p>
  <h1>Hiring is not a transaction</h1>
  <p class="ahead__sub">Both sides are choosing. Only one side usually behaves as though that’s true.</p>
  <div class="ahead__by">
    <span class="ahead__ph" id="byPh" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <span class="ahead__who"><b>Akshata Chandavarkar</b>Founder &amp; CEO</span>
  </div>
  <svg class="pglyph ahead__glyph" viewBox="0 0 420 62" preserveAspectRatio="none" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="202.8 31.5 200.0 36.9 197.1 42.8 190.6 48.3 184.5 49.6 175.4 50.6 170.0 47.6 162.2 43.3 159.5 36.9 157.8 30.7 159.1 24.3 164.1 17.7 169.5 13.1 177.1 11.8 184.2 10.9 190.7 13.7 196.0 17.4 199.9 24.0 201.8 30.9" fill="none" stroke="#B8B4AA" stroke-width="1.5" opacity="0.4" stroke-linecap="round"/><polyline points="233.7 31.9 233.1 37.7 229.0 43.1 223.3 49.0 216.5 50.9 208.5 50.7 200.6 47.7 194.9 44.1 191.7 38.7 190.3 31.2 191.2 23.8 196.1 18.2 201.5 13.5 208.7 11.0 215.1 11.3 222.9 14.4 229.7 17.8 231.7 24.1 233.9 31.8" fill="none" stroke="#B8B4AA" stroke-width="1.5" opacity="0.4" stroke-linecap="round"/><polyline points="217.4 31.3 216.6 36.9 212.7 43.4 207.2 48.8 199.3 51.4 191.8 49.6 185.1 48.6 179.3 44.6 176.3 37.0 174.6 31.7 175.8 23.2 179.0 17.7 185.0 13.6 191.9 11.6 200.8 10.8 207.3 14.7 213.6 17.3 217.3 24.2 218.9 31.1" fill="none" stroke="#DA0007" stroke-width="1.5" opacity="1" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
</div></header>

<section class="band band--w"><div class="wrap">
  <article class="art">
<p class="lede">A hire has two signatures on it. Almost every hiring process is designed as though it has one.</p>

<p>The candidate proves themselves repeatedly — CV, screen, panel, take-home, references. The company proves nothing. It describes itself in a job advertisement it wrote about the version of itself it would like to be, and the candidate is expected to take that on faith while being examined.</p>

<p>Then, eleven months later, someone leaves and it goes down as a bad hire.</p>

<h2>The asymmetry is not just unfair — it is inaccurate</h2>

<p>If you only assess one side, you can only predict one side. Everything that actually determines whether someone stays sits on the other one: how that manager makes decisions, what the team rewards, whether the work in the job description is the work anyone does.</p>

<p>None of it appears in the interview, because the interview is pointed the wrong way.</p>

<blockquote class="pull">If you only assess one side,
<em>you can only predict one side.</em></blockquote>

<h2>So we check the company too</h2>

<p>Before a request reaches a candidate, the team on the other end clears its own set of checks. Is the headcount funded. How many rounds, actually. How fast do they decide after the final interview. How long has the manager been in post. Did the last few people hired through us stay.</p>

<p>When something does not clear, the candidate still sees the request — with the flag attached and the reason written out. They decide whether it matters. We are not in a position to decide that for them, and pretending otherwise would be its own kind of disrespect.</p>

<h2>What this changes on the buying side</h2>

<p>It sounds like a candidate feature and it is mostly a client one. Teams that clear the checks get better response rates, because the people we approach have been told something true about them. Teams that do not clear get told which check failed, which is occasionally an unwelcome conversation and has more than once changed something before the search even started.</p>

<p>The most useful thing we do for some clients is not the shortlist. It is the sentence about why their last four hires left.</p>

<p class="signoff">Belong. Build. Become.</p></article>
